.TH AGENTBOX 1 "2026-08-10" "agentbox 0.0.1-dev" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
agentbox \- run an agentic coding tool in an isolated per-project environment
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B agentbox
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.RI [ ARGS ...]
.br
.B agentbox
.RI [ flags ]
.B \-\-
.I COMMAND-IN-BOX
.RI [ ARGS ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B agentbox
runs an agentic coding tool inside an isolated environment built for the
current project. It resolves the workspace root, merges layered
configuration, selects a backend satisfying the project's declared isolation
floor, builds an image matching the declared toolchains, constructs a masked
view of the workspace that hides secrets, starts a default-deny egress
proxy, and execs the agent with the working directory mapped to match your
shell's.
.PP
There is exactly
.B one box per workspace root.
The directory is the box's identity: there is no instance name, no default
box, and nothing to choose between. A project gets a second box by getting a
second root \(em
.B git worktree add ../feature
is a different directory, so it gets its own box, on its own branch, for
free.
.PP
The first non-flag argument is
.I interpreted:
if it names a reserved subcommand it dispatches to that subcommand;
otherwise the whole argument list is a command to run inside the box.
.B \-\-
forces the in-box interpretation. When an argument matches a reserved
subcommand but is not in first position, agentbox prints a note to stderr
suggesting
.BR \-\- .
.PP
The reserved set is closed and documented:
.PP
.RS
run shell up down status logs build config init rm version help
.RE
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.BR run " \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]"
Run a command in the box, creating and starting it if necessary. The
command's exit code is returned verbatim.
.TP
.B shell
Open an interactive shell in the box.
.TP
.B up
Create and start the box without running anything in it.
.TP
.B down
Stop the box and remove its guest and networks. The persistent home and the
box's identity survive, so a later
.B agentbox
brings back the same box rather than a fresh one.
.TP
.B rm
Remove the box and everything it holds: guest, networks, persistent home
volume, recorded state, and the image agentbox built for it. An image pinned
with
.B [image].ref
is never removed \(em agentbox reclaims only what it made. Pass
.B \-\-keep\-image
to retain a built image as well.
.TP
.B status
Print what this workspace's box is and what is in force for it: backend and
isolation tier, run state, image, mask mode and how many paths it hides,
egress mode and how many domains it allows, and any drift from current
configuration. Never creates a box.
.TP
.B logs
Stream the box's logs.
.B \-\-proxy
streams the egress proxy's log instead;
.B \-\-denied
filters it to refused requests;
.BR \-f / \-\-follow
follows.
.TP
.B build
Build or rebuild the image.
.B \-\-no\-cache
discards cached layers.
.TP
.B config
Print the effective merged configuration.
.B \-\-origin
annotates each key with the layer that set it.
.TP
.B init
Scaffold
.B agentbox.toml
and
.B .agentignore
in the workspace root. This is the only command that writes to the
workspace, and only on explicit invocation.
.TP
.B version
Print the agentbox version and each backend's availability.
.TP
.B help
Print usage.
.SH GLOBAL FLAGS
.TP
.BI \-C " DIR" "\fR, \fP" \-\-directory " DIR"
Resolve the workspace as if invoked from
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BI \-w " DIR" "\fR, \fP" \-\-workspace " DIR"
Override workspace-root detection.
.TP
.BI \-c " FILE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-config " FILE"
Use
.I FILE
as the workspace configuration layer, replacing any
.B agentbox.toml
found.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Use built-in defaults and flags only; skip the user and workspace layers.
.TP
.BI \-b " NAME" "\fR, \fP" \-\-backend " NAME"
Select a backend
.RB ( container " or " vm ).
A backend below the isolation floor is never selected.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-isolation " TIER"
Raise the isolation floor for this invocation. May raise but never lower;
lowering requires
.BR \-\-force\-isolation .
.TP
.BI \-\-force\-isolation " TIER"
Run below the project's declared isolation floor. Recorded in the box's
metadata and warned about on every subsequent invocation.
.TP
.BI \-e " KEY=VAL" "\fR, \fP" \-\-env " KEY=VAL"
Set an environment variable inside the box.
.TP
.BI \-E " KEY" "\fR, \fP" \-\-env\-passthrough " KEY"
Forward a host environment variable into the box by name.
.TP
.BI \-\-network " MODE"
Override the egress mode:
.BR off ", " proxy ", or " open .
.TP
.B \-\-rebuild
Rebuild the image before starting.
.TP
.B \-\-recreate
Recreate the box from current configuration, keeping its persistent home.
.TP
.B \-\-root
Run as uid 0 inside the box. Under the container tier this is uid 0 with
every capability dropped, and masking then relies on the guest lacking
CAP_SYS_ADMIN; agentbox warns when it applies.
.IP
Accepted after
.B run
and
.B shell
as well as before, but only ahead of the command:
.B agentbox run \-\-root mytool
runs mytool as root, while
.B agentbox run mytool \-\-root
passes
.B \-\-root
to mytool. Use
.B \-\-
to end agentbox's flags where the command's own first argument looks like
one.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mask
Disable secret masking for this invocation. Warns loudly. There is no
configuration key for this: turning masking off should require saying so at
the prompt, every time.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the full plan and change nothing. Building a plan never creates or
starts anything, so this is the same plan the enacting commands use.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output where supported. With
.B \-\-dry\-run
this is the inspection surface: it publishes the complete mask set, the
resolved egress policy, and the selected backend.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress non-essential diagnostics.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print additional diagnostics.
.SH WORKSPACE DETECTION
The workspace root is the nearest ancestor directory containing a version
control root or an
.B agentbox.toml
\(em searched upward from the current directory, or from
.B \-C
when given, and overridden entirely by
.BR \-w .
The resolved real path is what identifies the box, so two symlinks to one
directory address the same box.
.PP
The working directory inside the box mirrors your shell's position within
the workspace: running agentbox from a subdirectory lands you in the
corresponding subdirectory under
.BR [workspace].mount .
.SH STREAMS, SIGNALS, EXIT STATUS
Diagnostics go to
.BR stderr .
.B stdout
belongs to the in-box command, so agentbox output never corrupts a pipeline.
.PP
Signals are forwarded to the in-box process, including SIGWINCH so terminal
resizes propagate.
.PP
agentbox owns a small set of exit codes, per
.BR sysexits.h ;
any other status is the in-box command's, passed through verbatim.
.TP
.B 64
Usage error.
.TP
.B 69
No available backend satisfies the isolation floor. The diagnostic names
what is missing.
.TP
.B 70
Internal error.
.TP
.B 78
Invalid configuration, including unknown keys and backend-scope violations.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ./agentbox.toml
Workspace configuration layer.
.B .agentbox.toml
is accepted as an alternative; having both is an error.
.TP
.B ./.agentignore
Mask patterns, gitignore syntax.
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/agentbox/config.toml
User configuration layer.
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/agentbox/agentignore
User-global mask patterns, applied to every project.
.TP
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/agentbox/boxes/<key>/
Per-box state: metadata, generated proxy configuration, logs.
.TP
.B $XDG_CACHE_HOME/agentbox/build/
Generated build contexts, keyed by feature digest.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR agentbox.toml (5),
.BR agentbox-security (7)
